WebKit: the background responsiveness ping does not destroy the proxy it pings.

BackgroundProcessResponsivenessTimer::responsivenessCheckTimerFired pings the process with a
reference of its own. If the process is already gone the ping ends it and that reference is the last,
so the WebProcessProxy is destroyed inside its own timer callback, and destroying one aborts the UI
process on this port (crashDueToCheckedPtrToDeadObject, the family of patch 0021): The Verge, CNN
and NASA opened in a row did it every time. One reference is leaked per ping, as 0021 does for a
process that ended. The timer stays: it is what notices a page that spins while nothing is asked of it.

--- a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/BackgroundProcessResponsivenessTimer.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/BackgroundProcessResponsivenessTimer.cpp
@@ -91,6 +91,14 @@
     ASSERT(!m_timeoutTimer.isActive());
 
     m_timeoutTimer.startOneShot(responsivenessTimeout);
+    // LazyOS: the proxy outlives this call. When the process is already gone (a web process that exited
+    // cleanly, a service worker's) the ping ends it, and the reference `protect` takes below is then the
+    // last one: the proxy is destroyed inside this callback, and destroying a WebProcessProxy aborts the
+    // UI process on this port (crashDueToCheckedPtrToDeadObject; patch 0021). Seen every time after The
+    // Verge, CNN and NASA were opened in a row. One reference is leaked per ping (the interval doubles
+    // from 20 s, so a handful an hour); the ping is what finds a page that spins without being asked
+    // anything (the window's "not responding" bar), so the timer stays.
+    m_webProcessProxy->ref();
     protect(m_webProcessProxy)->send(Messages::WebProcess::BackgroundResponsivenessPing(), 0);
 }
 
